/* ===== glossary ===== */
GLOSSARY = [
  {t:"AI infrastructure", d:"The hardware and software systems used to support artificial intelligence work. It has special requirements: specialized processors rather than ordinary ones, storage that is both larger and faster than usual, and particular machine learning libraries and frameworks for the people building the models.", e:"A company that wants to train its own model discovers it cannot simply run it on the servers it already owns, and has to budget for a different class of machine entirely.", lo:"3.3"},
  {t:"application programming interface (API)", d:"An arrangement that lets one piece of software use a service provided by another without knowing how that service works inside. The consumer passes agreed-on values across and gets a result back, which is why software can be assembled out of parts built by different companies.", e:"A ride-hailing app sends a pickup point and a destination to a mapping service and receives the best route, without its developers ever having written a map.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"archival data", d:"Long-term copies of organizational data, kept mainly for compliance and reporting rather than for daily use. Because it is rarely read, it can live on slow, cheap, removable media, and it needs a life span measured in years rather than days.", e:"Internal email from several years ago that nobody expects to open, but that the organization must be able to produce if it is ever asked to.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"assembly language", d:"A very low-level programming language that lets a computer operate quickly and efficiently, and the language operating systems are often written in. Part of an operating system&rsquo;s job is to insulate everyone else from it.", e:"Dragging a file from a flash drive to a hard drive is one gesture for you and a long sequence of low-level instructions underneath, which you never have to see.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"backup data", d:"Short-term copies of organizational data, made so the organization can recover from a failure or disaster. Backups are frequently overwritten by newer ones, so their value is in being recent and quick to restore rather than in lasting.", e:"Last night&rsquo;s copy of the order system, held on fast storage so that if this morning&rsquo;s server fails the business is running again in minutes rather than days.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"bandwidth", d:"The transmission capacity of a computer or communications channel, measured in bits per second or multiples of it. It is how much data can reliably be carried in one second, and it is what decides whether a file takes eight seconds or a day and a half to arrive.", e:"The same video downloads in seconds over a fiber connection and takes hours over an old telephone modem, because only the capacity of the path changed.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"bit", d:"The smallest unit of data used by computers. Human communication is made of words; computer communication is made of bits, and everything sent over a network &mdash; a document, a song, a film &mdash; is bits, differing only in how many of them there are.", e:"A page of text runs to roughly fourteen thousand bytes while a print-quality photograph can be more than two hundred million, which is why one arrives instantly and the other does not.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"broad network access", d:"One of the five characteristics of cloud computing: because the services are reached over the internet, they are available from almost anywhere and from almost any web-enabled device. It is what lets a manager act on something without going back to an office.", e:"Approving an urgent order from a phone in an airport, using the same system that sits on the desktop back at work.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"campus area network", d:"A wide area network that spans several buildings belonging to one organization. It is a middle case between a network inside a single building and one that crosses a city or the world.", e:"A hospital group whose clinic, laboratory and administration buildings sit on one site and share one network across all three.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"capital expenditure and operational expenditure", d:"Two ways of paying for the same computing. A capital expenditure is money spent up front to own equipment; an operational expenditure is money spent continuously to use a service. Cloud computing converts one into the other, which changes the budget it comes out of and who has to approve it.", e:"Buying servers is a large purchase in one year; renting the same capacity is a monthly bill that rises and falls with how much the business actually used.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"cascading style sheets (CSS)", d:"The standard used to specify the formatting and layout of the elements on a web page. It separates how a page looks from what the page says, so the wording and the appearance can be changed independently.", e:"Changing one style rule makes every heading on a thousand-page site larger, without anyone editing a thousand pages.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"central processing unit (CPU)", d:"The general-purpose processor that performs a computer&rsquo;s calculations. It is built to handle instructions one after another very quickly, which suits ordinary software where each step depends on the result of the last one.", e:"Recalculating a spreadsheet, where each formula may need the answer from the formula above it before it can run.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"client", d:"Any computer or software application on a network that uses the services a server provides. Clients only request; they do not serve. A client usually has one user, whereas many users share the server it talks to.", e:"The mail program on a laptop asks a mail server for new messages; it never answers requests from anyone else.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"client-server network", d:"A network in which servers and clients have defined roles, some machines providing services and others only requesting them. It is the normal business arrangement, and nearly everyone at work is using one whether they know it or not.", e:"Every desk in an office opening files kept on a shared server rather than on each other&rsquo;s machines.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"cloud computing", d:"A model for enabling convenient, on-demand access over a network to a shared pool of configurable computing resources &mdash; networks, servers, storage, applications and services &mdash; that can be provisioned and released quickly with minimal management effort or interaction with the provider. In plainer words: computing rented by the hour from somebody else&rsquo;s building.", e:"A company that needs two hundred servers for a week gets them the same afternoon with a payment card and a web page, and stops paying when it stops using them.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"collocation facility", d:"A data center managed by a third party that rents space to several organizational customers. The tenant still owns and manages its own servers; what it stops paying for is the building, the power, the cooling and the physical security.", e:"A regional firm that puts its own machines in a rented, professionally run room instead of building a server room and a generator of its own.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"computer networking", d:"The sharing of data or services between machines. It needs three things and no fewer: a sender and a receiver with something to share, a pathway between them, and rules both sides follow.", e:"Two people can only have a conversation if there is something to say, a room to say it in, and a language they both speak; a network is the same three requirements in equipment.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"consumerization of IT", d:"The trend in which the devices people choose for themselves arrive at work and have to be integrated into the organization&rsquo;s infrastructure. It shifts part of the equipment decision away from the technology department and toward the individual.", e:"Employees expecting to read work email on their own phones, which the organization must then decide how to secure and support.", lo:"3.3"},
  {t:"content delivery network", d:"A network of servers in different geographical locations that store copies of the same content, so a request is answered by the copy nearest the user. It reduces the delay caused by distance, saves bandwidth, and is invisible to the person browsing.", e:"A video that plays instantly for viewers on three continents because each of them is actually being served from a machine a few hundred miles away.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"cyber-physical system", d:"A system that integrates computing with physical processes, coordinating software, sensors and mechanical components closely enough to monitor and control something in the real world with a high degree of autonomy. The consequences of a fault are physical, which is what makes securing them different.", e:"A factory where sensors, controllers and machines run most of the production line between them and reorganize the work without a person intervening.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"dark web", d:"Those areas of the web used for various criminal purposes, typically reachable only with specialized browsers that anonymize the user and hide their traces. It is deliberately hidden, and it is not the same thing as the deep web.", e:"A marketplace trading stolen payment card numbers, which no ordinary browser or search engine will take you to.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"database", d:"An organized collection of related data held so that it can be stored and retrieved reliably. The chapter treats databases as the thing hardware exists to run and storage exists to hold, which is why a storage decision is really a decision about how well questions can be answered later.", e:"A shop&rsquo;s record of every product, every supplier and every sale, kept once so that a price change is made in one place rather than in four spreadsheets.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"database management system", d:"The software that stores data, keeps it consistent, and answers questions about it. The chapter counts it as a third cost of capturing more data, alongside the storage space and the processing power, because somebody has to license, configure and run it.", e:"Two clerks record a sale at the same moment and the totals still agree afterwards, which is the software doing work nobody sees.", lo:"3.3"},
  {t:"data center", d:"Dedicated space set aside for an organization&rsquo;s infrastructure, ranging from a single server room to a building the size of a warehouse. Keeping the equipment in one place makes it easier to manage, repair, upgrade, power, cool and secure.", e:"A parcel carrier&rsquo;s facilities that can run for days on their own generated power, with cooling capacity comparable to that of a whole neighbourhood of houses.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"digital density", d:"The rising proportion of an organization&rsquo;s activity that exists as data and is carried out through connected devices. The chapter names it as the reason a well-functioning infrastructure has become essential rather than merely useful.", e:"A delivery that once left only a signed paper docket now leaves a scan at every handover, a location trace and a photograph, so the same journey produces a hundred times the record it used to.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"deep web", d:"Those parts of the web that conventional search engines cannot index: pages behind a login, pages generated from a database when somebody asks for them, and pages nothing links to. Most of the web is like this, and none of it is necessarily sinister.", e:"Your own course pages, your bank statements and an online store&rsquo;s product listings are all invisible to a search engine, for three different reasons.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"desktop virtualization", d:"Providing workers with a desktop environment that actually runs on a server rather than on the machine in front of them. It reduces the cost of licensing and maintaining software and helps with strict privacy and data protection duties, because the data never leaves the data center.", e:"A hospital where every screen shows the same working environment, and nothing is stored on the terminal itself if it is stolen.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"device driver", d:"Software that allows a computer to communicate with a particular piece of hardware. It is the translation layer between one general operating system and the specific behaviour of one make and model of device.", e:"Plugging in an unfamiliar printer and having the computer fetch the small piece of software that teaches it how to talk to that printer.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"domain name", d:"The part of a web address that helps a person recognize the company or individual it belongs to. Names exist because the numeric addresses machines actually use are hard to remember and can change.", e:"Seeing a familiar name in the address bar tells you whose server you have reached, which a string of numbers would not.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"e-business", d:"A business whose ordinary operations depend on internet technology rather than merely using it at the edges. The chapter&rsquo;s position is that almost any business today can be considered one, which is what makes infrastructure everyone&rsquo;s problem rather than the technology department&rsquo;s.", e:"A family bakery that takes orders on a website, buys flour through a supplier portal and rosters staff in a shared calendar is running on the internet even though nobody there would call it a technology company.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"edge computing", d:"What content delivery networks grew into: moving not only stored data but also the processing away from one central location out to the edges of the network, close to where the data is produced. The point is to cut the delay, and secondarily to cut traffic and cost.", e:"A monitoring device that decides for itself that a reading is dangerous, instead of sending every reading across the country and waiting for an answer.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"embedded system", d:"A computer designed to perform one specific set of tasks well, built into something that is not thought of as a computer. It has no general-purpose life; it does the one job.", e:"The computer that manages a car engine, a traffic light or a media player, which nobody ever installs software on.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"extranet", d:"A private part of the internet, cordoned off from ordinary users, that lets two or more firms do business together. The content is on the web, but only authorized partners can reach it after logging in, and it usually travels through a secured tunnel because the path itself is public.", e:"A supplier logging in to a manufacturer&rsquo;s system to see current orders, confirm shipments and update prices without anyone rekeying the data.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"field-programmable gate array (FPGA)", d:"A processor whose circuitry can be reconfigured after it is built, offering customizable hardware acceleration. It sits between a general-purpose processor and a chip designed for a single task.", e:"A team that needs one unusual calculation run very fast reshapes the hardware around that calculation instead of buying a chip that will never exist.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"file", d:"A block of data that stores specific content, such as a document, an image or a spreadsheet. It is the unit people name, move, share and delete, and it is the reason ordinary users never have to think about where anything physically sits.", e:"The report you saved yesterday, which you can find by its name without knowing anything about the disk it is on.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"five-nines availability", d:"Availability of 99.999 percent, which works out at just over five minutes of downtime in a year. It is the target data-intensive organizations aim for, and reaching it constrains everything from component design to the choice of site.", e:"A payment system that is expected to be unavailable for less time in a whole year than an average lunch break.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"folder", d:"A grouping of related files, shown as a folder in a graphical interface and called a directory underneath. Folders nest inside folders, which is how a large amount of digital content stays findable.", e:"Keeping coursework in one folder, then a folder per term inside it, then a folder per course inside that.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"frameworks and libraries", d:"Collections of prewritten code that developers assemble rather than write. They shorten development time, help one program run on several platforms, and supply the building blocks for specialized work such as machine learning.", e:"A small team shipping a working application in weeks because most of what it does was already written by somebody else and is being reused.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"generation of computing", d:"Each fundamental change in the way computing technologies work. The usual account runs from vacuum tubes through transistors and integrated circuits to low-cost multimedia machines, widespread internet access, ubiquitous mobile connectivity, and now artificial intelligence and quantum computing.", e:"The move from a room-sized machine that a few specialists operated to a phone in everyone&rsquo;s pocket is several such changes stacked on top of one another.", lo:"3.3"},
  {t:"graphical user interface (GUI)", d:"An interface that lets you instruct a computer by selecting or manipulating pictures, icons and menus rather than by typing commands. It is the reason using a computer no longer requires learning its language first.", e:"Dragging a file onto a disk icon instead of typing a copy instruction with the exact path of both places.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"graphics processing unit (GPU)", d:"A processor optimized for doing the same operation on very many pieces of data at once, using thousands of small cores. It was built for rendering images, where every pixel can be worked out independently, and turned out to suit machine learning for the same reason.", e:"Training a model, where enormous numbers of similar calculations have no need to wait for one another and can all run side by side.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"green computing", d:"Using computing resources more efficiently to reduce environmental impact, and using information systems to reduce environmental impact elsewhere. The chapter&rsquo;s tactics are consolidating underused machines, shifting work to shared providers, managing power on idle desktops, and printing less.", e:"Replacing a room of servers that each ran at a fraction of capacity with a few machines doing the same work, and paying less for power and cooling as a result.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"grid computing", d:"Combining the computing power of many smaller, independent, networked computers into one system in order to solve a problem that previously needed a supercomputer. Like cloud computing it uses distributed resources, but unlike cloud computing those resources are all applied to a single large problem.", e:"A simulation broken into thousands of small pieces, each finished by an ordinary desktop machine somewhere and sent back to be reassembled.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"hardware", d:"The physical machines that run the applications and databases an organization needs in order to process transactions and analyze business data. Because organizations perform many different kinds of activity, they need several different kinds of computer to support them.", e:"The register at the front of a shop, the server in the back room and the phone in the manager&rsquo;s pocket are all hardware doing three different jobs.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"host name", d:"The part of a web address naming the particular server, or group of servers, that will answer the request. The prefix www usually means the default site, and larger organizations use other host names for their separate services.", e:"One organization&rsquo;s mail service, map service and document storage each answer on a different host name in front of the same domain.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"HTML tag", d:"A code set apart with angle brackets that tells a browser how part of a document should be presented, or what that part means. Most tags come in pairs, one to start an element and one to end it.", e:"A pair of tags around a phrase can make it bold, mark it as a heading, or turn it into a link to another document.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"HTML5", d:"The chapter&rsquo;s name for the current version of the web&rsquo;s markup language, which added ways of structuring a page and of presenting modern multimedia without extra software. Treat it as a label for the modern language rather than as a version number still being counted up, because the markup is now revised continuously rather than released in numbered editions.", e:"Video that plays in the browser itself, with no separate plug-in to install, because the page can simply say that this is a video.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"HTTPS", d:"The secure version of the web&rsquo;s transfer protocol, now used routinely for anything sensitive because data travelling between a server and a browser can otherwise be intercepted and read. The padlock in the address bar is the browser reporting that it is in use.", e:"Entering a card number on a page without the padlock means the number crosses several unknown networks in a form anyone along the way could read.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"hybrid cloud", d:"An arrangement in which an organization keeps some applications and data in-house and moves others to a public cloud. The chapter presents it as what organizations usually settle on, because no single provider meets every need and some data is not worth moving.", e:"A firm running its public website on rented capacity while its patient records stay on machines it owns and controls.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"hyperlink", d:"A reference from one document to another, which the reader can follow. Links are what turn a collection of separate pages into a web rather than a library of unconnected documents.", e:"Clicking a word in an article and arriving at the source it was quoting, on a completely different organization&rsquo;s server.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"hypertext", d:"The key feature of the web: documents that contain links to other documents, so a reader can move between them in any order rather than front to back. Everything else about the web is built on top of this one idea.", e:"Reading three paragraphs of one page, following a link, and finishing on a page written by somebody who has never heard of the first author.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"Hypertext Markup Language (HTML)", d:"The standard method of specifying the structure and content of web pages. It is a text-based format that uses codes called tags to tell the browser what each part of the document is and how it should be presented.", e:"Choosing to view the source of any page shows the same words you were reading, wrapped in codes that gave them their headings, links and emphasis.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP)", d:"The protocol web servers use to process requests for pages. It is the agreed set of rules by which a browser asks for a document and a server answers, and it is the reason a browser made by one company can read a page served by another.", e:"Typing an address and pressing return is a request in this protocol, whether the server at the other end runs the same software as your machine or not.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"infrastructure as a service (IaaS)", d:"The cloud service model in which only the basic capabilities of processing, storage and networking are provided. The customer has the most control and the most responsibility: it still chooses the operating system, manages its software licences, and bears relatively high setup costs.", e:"A streaming company renting raw computing capacity to convert films into different formats and to run its own website on top of it.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"information systems infrastructure", d:"The hardware, system software, storage, networking and data centers an organization needs in order to operate fully and to meet its goals. It is the foundation everything else in the organization is built on, and the chapter&rsquo;s point is that it creates value by enabling efficiency, effectiveness and increasingly agility.", e:"Nobody buys a bank because of its servers, and every service the bank sells stops the moment those servers do.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"input device", d:"Hardware that supplies data to a computer. Keyboards, mice, touch screens and cameras are the familiar ones, and sensors have widened the category enormously.", e:"A scanner at a receiving dock, which is doing nothing more than getting a number into a system without anybody typing it.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"internet", d:"A large worldwide collection of networks that use a common protocol to communicate with one another. It is not one network but an agreement that lets many separately owned networks behave as though they were one.", e:"A message from a home connection to an office server crosses several companies&rsquo; equipment on the way, and nobody involved had to arrange it in advance.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"Internet of Things (IoT)", d:"A vast network of interconnected physical devices that collect and share data, often producing very large amounts of information that can be gathered and analyzed centrally. It extends the reach of information systems to objects that were never previously connected to anything.", e:"Refrigerated containers reporting their temperature continuously across a supply chain, so a spoiled shipment is known about before it is opened.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"internetworking", d:"Connecting host computers and their networks together to form larger networks. The word internet is a contraction of it, and it describes exactly what the internet does.", e:"A company network, a university network and a home network all reaching each other without any of them merging into a single owner&rsquo;s system.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"intranet", d:"An internal site that looks and behaves like a public website and uses the same technologies, but uses authentication or firewalls so that only authorized employees can see it. In its simplest form the traffic never leaves the organization at all.", e:"The internal pages where staff read announcements, take required training and change their own benefit elections without contacting anyone.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"IP address", d:"The address of a computer or device that enables a network to route messages to the right destination. The older form is four numbers between 0 and 255 separated by dots; the newer, much larger form exists because the world ran short of the older kind.", e:"Every device on a home network has one, which is how the router knows which machine asked for the page that just came back.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"lean startup methodology", d:"An approach built on rapid cycles of devising a solution, building the smallest version of it that can be tested, and finding out quickly whether it has the effect it was supposed to have. It matters for infrastructure because it means committing to equipment before the answer is known makes no sense.", e:"Putting a rough version of a new service in front of real customers for a month before deciding whether it deserves a permanent system.", lo:"3.3"},
  {t:"local area network (LAN)", d:"A network for sharing data, applications and other resources between several users, typically within one building. It is the ordinary office or home network, and its capacity is usually far higher than the connection leaving the building.", e:"Everyone in an office printing to the same printer and opening the same shared drive, all without anything crossing the internet.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"mainframe", d:"A large computer used as the main central computing system of a major organization, optimized for high availability, heavy use and security. It is the machine of choice for mission-critical work such as processing very large volumes of transactions.", e:"A bank whose account balances all live on one heavily protected system that thousands of tellers and cash machines use at the same time.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"measured service", d:"One of the five characteristics of cloud computing: the provider monitors usage and the customer pays only for what it uses. Fixed costs become variable costs that are easy to track &mdash; and easy to run up if nobody is tracking them.", e:"A bill that is small in a quiet month, large in a busy one, and catastrophic in a month when somebody else was using your account.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"metropolitan area network", d:"A wide area network covering the area of a city. It is larger than a network in one building or on one site, and smaller than a network spanning a country or the world.", e:"A city transit authority connecting stations, depots and its control room across the whole urban area.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"microservices", d:"The small building blocks that packaged business capabilities are made of on a technical level. Each does one narrow job and communicates with the others across defined interfaces, so one can be replaced without rebuilding the rest.", e:"Changing how a company calculates delivery charges without touching the part of the system that takes payment.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"minimum viable product (MVP)", d:"The smallest version of a solution that can be put in front of real users to find out whether it has the desired effect. It exists to produce an answer cheaply, not to be the finished thing.", e:"A single page and a form, launched to see whether anybody signs up, before a system is built behind it.", lo:"3.3"},
  {t:"mobile device", d:"Tablets and smartphones, which have become part of an organization&rsquo;s information systems infrastructure rather than a personal convenience beside it. They have one user, travel everywhere, and are increasingly where work is actually done.", e:"A field technician who receives the day&rsquo;s jobs, records what was done and takes payment on the same handheld device.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"modular architecture", d:"Building software by choosing individual blocks best suited to a particular need and connecting them through interfaces, rather than writing one large program that contains everything. It buys flexibility at the cost of having to plan, select and orchestrate the pieces.", e:"Assembling a service out of separate components for orders, inventory, customers and payments, each of which can be swapped later.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"monolithic application", d:"An application built as a single unit that includes all the features it needs, with the whole thing in one code base. That makes it relatively easy to develop, test and deploy, and correspondingly hard to change one part of without disturbing the rest.", e:"A long-serving system where adding one field to a form means retesting and redeploying the entire program.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"Moore&rsquo;s law", d:"The prediction, made in 1965 by one of Intel&rsquo;s cofounders, that the number of transistors on a chip would keep doubling at regular intervals. It is an observation and an industry roadmap rather than a law of nature, and its pace has slowed, with recent gains coming increasingly from adding more cores that work in parallel.", e:"The first processors held a few thousand transistors and current ones hold billions, which is why a phone outperforms the machines that once filled a room.", lo:"3.3"},
  {t:"neuromorphic chip", d:"A processor whose design is inspired by the architecture of the brain, aiming to improve energy efficiency and speed for artificial intelligence work. It is one of several attempts to escape the power cost of doing this work on general-purpose hardware.", e:"Research hardware that tries to do recognition tasks at a small fraction of the electricity a conventional processor would need.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"on-demand self-service", d:"One of the five characteristics of cloud computing: a customer can obtain resources as needed, without lengthy negotiation and in many cases without speaking to anyone at the provider at all. It is what makes an experiment possible this afternoon rather than next quarter.", e:"Setting up new servers through a web control panel with a payment card, and having them running before the meeting that requested them has ended.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"operating system", d:"The most prominent kind of system software. It coordinates the interaction between hardware components, attached devices, application software and users, and it provides a common layer so that programs can be written for the operating system rather than for every individual model of machine.", e:"The reason a word processor ships in one version for one operating system rather than a separate version for every laptop ever made.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"operational data", d:"Data used to manage business processes as they happen, such as processing transactions, or to analyze the business. It needs to be current and fast to reach, which is why it lives on the quickest storage an organization has.", e:"The stock level a shopping site checks the instant you add something to a basket, which would be worthless if it took a minute to read.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"output device", d:"Hardware that presents results to a person. Monitors, printers and speakers are the familiar examples, and the category matters because a system nobody can read the results of has not finished its job.", e:"A picking list printed in a warehouse, which is where the computer&rsquo;s decision finally becomes something a person can act on.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"packaged business capability (PBC)", d:"A building block designed to perform one well-specified business function, such as processing payments or managing orders. Different vendors offer competing blocks for the same function, so an organization can choose the one that best fits and connect it to the others.", e:"Using a specialist payments component inside your own ordering system instead of writing payment handling yourself.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"packet", d:"One of the small chunks a request or a file is broken into for transmission. Breaking data up allows it to be routed and moved efficiently, and the pieces are reassembled at the far end before anyone sees them.", e:"A photograph you send arrives as thousands of fragments that may take different paths and are put back in order at the other end.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"peer", d:"Any computer that may both request and provide services, rather than being only a client or only a server. Peer arrangements are common in small offices and homes, where no machine is dedicated to serving the others.", e:"Two laptops sharing a folder directly with each other, with neither of them being the office server.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"peer-to-peer network (P2P)", d:"A network that lets any computer or device on it both provide and request services. It needs no dedicated server, which makes it cheap and simple, and correspondingly harder to control centrally.", e:"A household where any machine can reach the shared printer and the shared drive without a server sitting between them.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"peripheral", d:"A device attached to a computer, such as a printer, whose interaction with everything else is coordinated by the operating system. From the software&rsquo;s point of view a peripheral is something the operating system knows how to reach.", e:"Any application can print, because the operating system rather than each program deals with the printer.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"personal area network (PAN)", d:"Wireless communication between devices over a very short range, under about ten meters, using technologies such as Bluetooth. It is the smallest of the network types and the one most people use without noticing.", e:"Headphones connected to a phone, or a laptop connected to a wireless keyboard on the same desk.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"personal computer", d:"A computer used for personal computing and small business computing, serving one user at a time. It is the reference point against which the other classes of computer are usually described.", e:"The machine on an office desk, which does one person&rsquo;s work and would be overwhelmed if a thousand people asked it questions at once.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"planned obsolescence", d:"Designing a product to last only for a certain life span. In hardware it appears as components that cannot be serviced, so the device is replaced when one fails; in software it appears as older versions that cannot open newer files, or support that is simply withdrawn.", e:"A vendor announcing an end-of-support date, which turns a working fleet of machines into an upgrade decision whether the organization wanted one or not.", lo:"3.3"},
  {t:"platform as a service (PaaS)", d:"The cloud service model in which the provider supplies the computing platform &mdash; operating system, web server, database management system and development tools &mdash; and the customer runs its own applications on it. The customer controls the application and little else, and stops buying platform licences.", e:"A team deploying software it wrote onto a rented platform, without anyone on that team ever installing or patching a database engine.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"private cloud", d:"A cloud internal to one organization, which still provides self-service access to pooled resources on a pay-for-use basis but keeps everything inside the organization&rsquo;s own control. It does not remove the work of running an infrastructure; it buys customizability, control and privacy instead.", e:"A large firm letting its own departments provision servers from a shared internal pool in minutes, rather than from an outside provider.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"programmable logic controller (PLC)", d:"A specialized computing device used to automate machines and processes, from a factory line to a ski lift. It is built to run one control task reliably for years in conditions no office machine would survive.", e:"The device that decides when a conveyor starts and stops, which has no screen, no keyboard and no user.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"protocol", d:"A definition of the procedures computers follow when they transmit and receive data. It is the agreed language of a network, and without it a message can arrive perfectly and still mean nothing to the receiver.", e:"An email that reaches you in a language you cannot read has travelled the whole way successfully and still failed to communicate.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"public cloud", d:"Cloud services that any interested party can use on a pay-per-use basis. It suits work that needs to scale rapidly, and organizations that do not have the capital or the people to build capacity of their own.", e:"A new company launching a service worldwide on rented capacity, having bought no equipment at all.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"QR code", d:"A two-dimensional bar code with a high storage capacity, usually holding a web address so that scanning it with a camera opens a page. It can also trigger an action, such as starting a call, sending a message, or making a payment.", e:"A code on a table in a restaurant that opens the menu on your phone, with no address to type and no application to install.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"radio frequency identification (RFID)", d:"A technology that uses electromagnetic fields to transmit data between a reader and a small tag attached to an object. Unlike a bar code it does not need line of sight or hand scanning, can hold more data, and can be read at a distance.", e:"A pallet passing through a doorway and being counted automatically, without anyone pointing a scanner at each carton.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"rapid elasticity", d:"One of the five characteristics of cloud computing: resources can be scaled up or down almost instantly, often automatically, in step with demand. Equipment ordinarily takes weeks to arrive and days to configure, which is why owning means guessing in advance.", e:"A retailer whose capacity grows through a holiday rush and shrinks again in January, without a single purchase order.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"resource pooling", d:"One of the five characteristics of cloud computing: the provider manages distributed resources that are assigned dynamically to many customers, so a customer rents capacity without knowing which machine or which building it came from. Providers often do allow a region to be specified.", e:"A European company insisting its storage stay on servers in Europe, which is a data protection decision rather than a technical one.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"RFID tag", d:"The small transponder attached to an object so it can be identified by a reader. Passive tags cost a few cents and are read from a few feet away; active tags contain a battery, cost several dollars, and can be read from hundreds of feet.", e:"A tag under an animal&rsquo;s skin carrying an owner&rsquo;s details, and a tag on a shipping container carrying a manufacturing history, are the same technology at two sizes.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"root directory", d:"The top of a file hierarchy, typically the drive itself, from which every subdirectory branches out. It is the reason a location can be described as a path from a known starting point.", e:"Following a chain of folders from the drive down to one document is walking the tree from the root to a leaf.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"scalability", d:"The ability to adapt to increases or decreases in demand for processing or data storage. It is one of the main promises of cloud computing, and not every provider can meet every organization&rsquo;s idea of it, so it is something to test rather than assume.", e:"A ticket site that stays up when a popular event goes on sale, because capacity grew in the minutes when it was needed.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"sensor", d:"An input technology that collects data about the physical environment &mdash; temperature, pressure, vibration, proximity &mdash; and supplies it in real time. Sensors are what let information systems observe the world rather than only record what people type.", e:"A vibration sensor on a motor that reports a change in behaviour days before the motor fails.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"server", d:"Any computer on a network that makes files, printing, communications and other services available to other users of the network. Servers are optimized for many simultaneous users, so they carry more processing power, memory, storage and network capacity than a single-user machine.", e:"One machine answering thousands of visitors at once, some of them getting the identical page and others getting a page assembled for them individually.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"serverless computing", d:"A newer platform arrangement that frees the customer from setting up machines at all: small, specific functions are run by the provider on demand, scaled automatically, and billed by the time they actually run. The servers still exist; the customer just never meets them.", e:"A function that resizes an uploaded photograph, which costs nothing on a day when nobody uploads anything.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"service-level agreement (SLA)", d:"A contract specifying the level of service to be provided in terms of performance, warranties and disaster recovery. It does not guarantee that resources will be available; it promises service levels and provides refunds or discounts when they are missed, which makes it mainly a way of settling disputes.", e:"A refund covering the fee for the hours a service was down, which does not cover the orders the business lost while it was down.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"software as a service (SaaS)", d:"The cloud service model in which the customer uses only the application, with no knowledge of or control over the infrastructure beneath it and usually limited ability to configure it. It is the easiest model to adopt because nothing has to be maintained or updated by the customer.", e:"Company email, shared documents, meetings and customer records that staff simply log in to, with nobody in the building patching anything.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"supercomputer", d:"The most expensive and most powerful class of computer, used mainly for massive scientific problems rather than by business organizations. Even at the top of the range some problems take a year to compute, which is what motivated spreading work across many ordinary machines instead.", e:"Modelling a climate scenario or a nuclear reaction, work that no ordinary machine could finish in a useful amount of time.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"system software", d:"The collection of programs that control the basic operations of computer hardware. Application software does not talk to hardware directly; it talks to the system software, which talks to the machine.", e:"A spreadsheet asks to save a file and never has to know what kind of drive is fitted or how it is wired.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"tensor processing unit (TPU)", d:"A processor custom-designed to optimize the kind of matrix calculations that dominate deep learning. It is more specialized than a graphics processor and correspondingly faster on the work it was built for.", e:"Hardware built by a large provider specifically to run its own language models, and then rented out to customers doing similar work.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"thin client", d:"A machine with minimal memory, storage and processing capability, used as a window onto software that actually runs elsewhere. It is cheap, replaceable, and holds nothing worth stealing.", e:"A terminal in a public area that has no data on it at all, so losing it costs the price of the box and nothing else.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"top-level domain", d:"The suffix at the end of a domain name. Some indicate a kind of organization, such as commercial, educational, non-profit or government; more than two hundred are two-letter country codes; and a growing number of generic ones name particular industries or uses.", e:"The last part of an address tells you something about who registered it, which is why an unexpected suffix on a familiar-looking name is worth a second look.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"transmission control protocol/internet protocol (TCP/IP)", d:"The protocol suite the internet uses to move web pages and other information. It breaks a request into packets, routes them, reassembles them at the destination, and does the same in reverse with the reply.", e:"Every page you open is this cycle running twice: a request out in pieces, a page back in pieces, reassembled before you see anything.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"transmission media", d:"The physical pathway &mdash; cables and wireless &mdash; used to carry data. It is the second of the three requirements of a network, and the one that most directly limits how much can be sent and how quickly.", e:"The difference between a fiber line and an old copper connection is nothing but the pathway, and it changes a download from seconds to hours.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"uniform resource locator (URL)", d:"The address people actually use to identify and locate a web page. It names the host, the domain and the top-level domain, and usually a path to the particular resource wanted within that site.", e:"A long address ending in a series of words after a slash is naming a specific page inside a site, not just the site itself.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"utility computing", d:"The model cloud computing uses: renting processing, storage or networking from an outside provider as needed and paying only for what is actually used, the way a household pays for electricity. It is where the shift from buying capacity to buying service comes from.", e:"Paying for computing by the hour that it ran, rather than paying once for a machine that then sits idle most nights.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"virtual private network (VPN)", d:"A technology that lets someone outside the office connect securely to the organization&rsquo;s internal network across the public internet. It is what makes an intranet reachable from home or on the road without putting it on the open web.", e:"Working from home and reaching the same internal systems as a colleague at a desk, over a connection that is encrypted the whole way.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"virtualization", d:"Running several separate computing environments on one physical machine, so that many underused servers can be consolidated onto a few well-used ones. It is the technique underneath both desktop virtualization and most of what cloud providers sell.", e:"Twenty servers that each ran at a fraction of capacity replaced by three that run properly loaded, with a large fall in power and cooling cost.", lo:"3.4"},
  {t:"Voice over IP (VoIP)", d:"Technologies that use internet infrastructure to place telephone or video calls. Their existence is the clearest sign that computing and telecommunications have converged: the call and the web page now travel the same way.", e:"A team meeting held over an internet application rather than a conference call, with the organization&rsquo;s telephone system quietly becoming software.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"web browser", d:"A software application used to locate and display web pages, including their text, graphics and multimedia. It requests documents, interprets the markup it receives, and decides how the result looks on your screen.", e:"The same page looks slightly different on two machines because the browser, not the server, does the final drawing.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"web page", d:"A hypertext document containing both content and links to other documents. It is the unit the web is made of, and the thing a web server actually sends when it is asked for something.", e:"An article you are reading, together with the links inside it that would take you somewhere else entirely.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"web server", d:"A computer that stores web pages and processes requests for them. Many of them do not store finished pages at all: they run a program that assembles the page when it is asked for, often by querying a database.", e:"A product page that did not exist a second before you asked for it, because it was built from stored rows about that product.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"website", d:"A collection of interlinked web pages owned by the same organization or individual, usually hosted on a web server. The site is the unit an organization publishes; the page is the unit a visitor reads.", e:"One organization&rsquo;s address covering thousands of pages, all reachable from each other and all under the same name.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"Wi-Fi network", d:"The everyday name for a wireless local area network, which connects devices using high-frequency radio waves instead of cables. Ease of installation made it the default in offices, homes and public places.", e:"A cafe where a dozen strangers are all on one network that took an afternoon and one box to install.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"wide area network (WAN)", d:"A network connecting multiple local area networks over a large distance, often with ownership and management distributed among several parties. It ranges from a few buildings to a city to the whole world, and the internet is the largest example of one.", e:"A retailer whose stores, warehouses and head office are all on one network although no single organization owns every link between them.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"wireless local area network (WLAN)", d:"A local area network that uses high-frequency radio waves rather than cables, installed to connect mobile devices or where running cable is impractical. It is the same idea as a wired local network with the pathway changed.", e:"A warehouse where handheld scanners work anywhere in the building because no cable could reach a moving person.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"workstation", d:"A computer built for medical, engineering, architectural, animation or graphics work, optimized for visualizing and rendering three-dimensional models. It typically serves one user, but with far more processing power, memory and graphics capability than an ordinary desktop.", e:"The machine an architect uses to walk a client through a building that has not been built, which would defeat an ordinary office computer.", lo:"3.2"},
  {t:"World Wide Web", d:"A system of interlinked documents on the internet, and in effect a graphical interface to it. The web is not the internet: the internet is the network of networks, and the web is one very successful thing running on top of it.", e:"Email, video calls and file transfers all use the internet without being the web at all, which is why the two words are not interchangeable.", lo:"3.2"}
];
